CAT = "aatl2Systems"; SEG = "SYS"
Q = [
("Which document does a supplier send each month listing the invoices, credit notes and payments on a customer's account?",
 "Statement of account", ["Remittance advice", "Credit note", "Purchase order"],
 "A statement of account summarises the account for the period and shows the balance owed. A remittance advice goes the other way, from the customer, to say what a payment covers."),
("Hollins Ltd pays three of a supplier's invoices by BACS and emails a note naming them. The note is a:",
 "Remittance advice", ["Statement of account", "Petty cash voucher", "Purchase order"],
 "The remittance advice tells the seller which invoices to clear, so the receipt can be allocated correctly in the receivables ledger."),
("Which book of prime entry records credit notes issued to customers for goods they sent back?",
 "Sales returns daybook", ["Purchases returns daybook", "Sales daybook", "Discounts allowed daybook"],
 "Credit notes for returns from customers go in the sales returns daybook. Returns the business sends to its own suppliers go in the purchases returns daybook."),
("A customer pays in time to take a prompt payment discount, and the seller issues a credit note for it. The credit note is recorded in the:",
 "Discounts allowed daybook", ["Sales returns daybook", "Discounts received daybook", "Sales daybook"],
 "Credit notes for prompt payment discount given to customers are listed in the discounts allowed daybook. Discounts received are the ones suppliers give the business."),
("Where is the individual account of each credit customer kept?",
 "Receivables ledger", ["General ledger", "Payables ledger", "Sales daybook"],
 "The receivables ledger (sales ledger) holds one account per credit customer. The general ledger holds the receivables ledger control account, which totals them."),
("What is the main purpose of a trial balance?",
 "To check debits equal credits", ["To find the year's profit", "To list each customer's balance", "To prove the bank balance"],
 "A trial balance lists every general ledger balance to check that total debits equal total credits. It is a step towards the financial statements, not a profit figure itself."),
("Which financial statement shows a business's assets, liabilities and capital at one date?",
 "Statement of financial position", ["Statement of profit or loss", "Trial balance", "Cash book"],
 "The statement of financial position is a snapshot at one date. The statement of profit or loss covers income and expenses for a period."),
("A sole trader's business has assets of £84,500 and owes £19,300 to suppliers and the bank. Its capital is:",
 "£65,200", ["£103,800", "£64,200", "£84,500"],
 "Capital = assets - liabilities = £84,500 - £19,300 = £65,200. Adding the two figures gives £103,800, which is not how the equation works."),
("A business buys office furniture on credit. What is the dual effect?",
 "Assets up, liabilities up", ["Assets up, capital up", "Assets down, liabilities up", "Expenses up, assets down"],
 "The furniture is a new asset, and the amount owed to the supplier is a new liability. Capital is unchanged because the owner has put nothing in."),
("How is the monthly rent a business pays for its office classified?",
 "Expense", ["Asset", "Liability", "Capital"],
 "Rent is a cost of running the business for the period, so it is an expense. It would only be an asset if it were paid in advance for a later period."),
("A five-year bank loan taken out by the business is classified as a:",
 "Liability", ["Asset", "Expense", "Income"],
 "The loan is money the business owes, so it is a liability. The cash received from it is the asset."),
("Customer accounts are coded with the first three letters of the name and a number, such as HAR014. This type of code is:",
 "Alphanumerical", ["Alphabetical", "Numerical", "A product code"],
 "A code that mixes letters and digits is alphanumerical. An alphabetical code uses letters only and a numerical code digits only."),
("Which is a benefit of a digital bookkeeping system over a manual one?",
 "Daybook totals post to ledgers automatically", ["Every entry is checked and guaranteed correct", "Recurring entries never need checking", "Duplicate entries become impossible"],
 "Software transfers daybook data to the ledgers and control accounts for you. It cannot stop a wrong figure being keyed in, and a manual entry can duplicate an automated one."),
("A monthly direct debit is set up as a recurring entry in accounting software. It rises from £85 to £92, but nobody updates the entry. What happens?",
 "£85 keeps being posted", ["The software posts £92", "The entry stops posting", "The bank rejects the payment"],
 "A recurring entry posts the stored amount until someone changes it, so the books show £85 while the bank pays £92. The difference only shows up when the bank is reconciled."),
("Which of these can a digital bookkeeping system usually import directly?",
 "A CSV file of bank transactions", ["A paper remittance advice", "A handwritten petty cash voucher", "A customer's telephone order"],
 "Software can import bank records, CSV files and data from third-party software. Paper and spoken information still has to be keyed in."),
("A credit sale is posted to the wrong customer's account in the receivables ledger. What is the most likely consequence?",
 "The wrong customer is asked to pay", ["The trial balance will not balance", "Total sales are overstated", "VAT on the sale is understated"],
 "The total of the receivables ledger is unchanged, so the trial balance still balances. But one customer's statement shows a debt they do not owe, and the other's misses one."),
]
